    {
      "id": "NAMCO246",
      "label": "NAMCO System 246",
      "when": [
        "system246"
      ],
      "virtualWidth": 640,
      "virtualHeight": 448,
      "extensions": {},
      "_extensions_note": "Nothing is claimed by extension. An arcade game is a BUNDLE - a security dongle, the media, a boot program - and a lone file dropped on the frontend cannot be one, so these machines are reached through a project rather than by opening a rom. A .iso opened on its own is a PlayStation 2 disc, which is what it looks like.",
      "input": {
        "name": "Arcade Panel",
        "_comment": "ONE panel, covering every layout the fifty-five System 246 and 256 games were built with, because a declaration is static and cannot follow a project's settings. A cabinet is not a console: there are no controller ports, there is a JVS I/O board with a loom going to whatever that cabinet had - a fight stick, a wheel and three pedals, a light gun, a Taiko drum, twin levers, a touch panel - and the board never reads the bits its own loom has not got. Buttons 1 to 6 are the JVS switch bits of those names, and which face button each is depends on the game (Tekken's four are 1, 2, 4 and 5; Soul Calibur's are 1, 2, 3 and 4), which is a fact about the cabinet rather than a choice: waterbox/arcade/ACJV.cpp carries the per-game wiring, keyed by the arcade_game setting. Coin 1 and Coin 2 are the COIN MECH, so they are counted on the press and not held - one press is one credit. The drum's eight names are the eight piezo sensors; the lever names are Zoids' twin sticks. Test mode is NOT here: on this board it is a DIP switch on the I/O board rather than a button, so it is a setting the project pins and the movie cites. Every control declared is a column in every movie this machine records, which is why the DIP switches stayed out of the list.",
