  "settings": [
    {
      "name": "machine",
      "display": "Machine",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "ps2",
        "system246",
        "system256",
        "system256super"
      ],
      "default": "ps2",
      "description": "Which of this package's machines the project is. 'ps2' is the PlayStation 2 console. The other three are NAMCO's arcade boards. These ARE PlayStation 2s: a Sony COH-H motherboard with a NAMCO board in the expansion bay. They need a different kind of project. It has an arcade BIOS from a COH-H board and not a retail one, the game's security dongle, its media and a boot program, each as a file of its own kind, and it has a JVS control panel in place of the controller ports. 'system246' runs the main processor (the EE) at the console's 294.912 MHz. 'system256' runs it a third faster (393.216 MHz) and 'system256super' a half faster (442.368 MHz). The clock speed is the difference between those two boards, and a game recorded on one does not play back on the other. The machine decides the whole project: its inputs, its files, its settings and its firmware. A movie made on one machine does not run on another."
    },
    {
      "name": "port1",
      "display": "Controller 1 Type",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "dualshock2",
        "guitar",
        "jogcon",
        "negcon",
        "popn",
        "guncon2"
      ],
      "default": "dualshock2",
      "description": "What is plugged into port 1. 'dualshock2' is the standard controller, which almost all games expect. The special controllers are for the games made for them. 'guitar' is the Guitar Hero controller (a strum bar, five frets, a whammy bar and a tilt sensor). 'jogcon' is Ridge Racer's dial with force feedback. 'negcon' is Namco's twisting pad. 'popn' is the nine buttons of Pop'n Music. They are offered only on the two ports of the console itself.\n\n'guncon2' is Namco's light gun, for Time Crisis and the dozen or so games like it. It is not a controller. It plugs into the console's USB socket with this same number, so choosing it leaves the CONTROLLER socket empty, as on a real console with a light gun. The number matters as well as the choice, because a few games look for the gun only on USB port 2. The place the gun points at is stored in the movie as two ordinary axes, so a recording plays back anywhere. The mouse is not read.\n\nIt is part of the machine. A game asks what is connected, so two projects that differ here are different machines and cannot share movies.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "ps2"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "port2",
      "display": "Controller 2 Type",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "dualshock2",
        "guitar",
        "jogcon",
        "negcon",
        "popn",
        "guncon2"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "What is plugged into port 2. 'dualshock2' is the standard controller, which almost all games expect. The special controllers are for the games made for them. 'guitar' is the Guitar Hero controller (a strum bar, five frets, a whammy bar and a tilt sensor). 'jogcon' is Ridge Racer's dial with force feedback. 'negcon' is Namco's twisting pad. 'popn' is the nine buttons of Pop'n Music. They are offered only on the two ports of the console itself.\n\n'guncon2' is Namco's light gun, for Time Crisis and the dozen or so games like it. It is not a controller. It plugs into the console's USB socket with this same number, so choosing it leaves the CONTROLLER socket empty, as on a real console with a light gun. The number matters as well as the choice, because a few games look for the gun only on USB port 2. The place the gun points at is stored in the movie as two ordinary axes, so a recording plays back anywhere. The mouse is not read.\n\nIt is part of the machine. A game asks what is connected, so two projects that differ here are different machines and cannot share movies.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "ps2"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "port3",
      "display": "Controller 3 Type",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "dualshock2"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "What is plugged into slot B of the multitap in port 1. It needs Multitap in Port 1 to be on. 'dualshock2' is the standard controller, which almost all games expect.\n\nIt is part of the machine. A game asks the console what is connected, so two projects that differ here are different machines and cannot share movies.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "ps2"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "port4",
      "display": "Controller 4 Type",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "dualshock2"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "What is plugged into slot C of the multitap in port 1. It needs Multitap in Port 1 to be on. 'dualshock2' is the standard controller, which almost all games expect.\n\nIt is part of the machine. A game asks the console what is connected, so two projects that differ here are different machines and cannot share movies.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "ps2"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "port5",
      "display": "Controller 5 Type",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "dualshock2"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "What is plugged into slot D of the multitap in port 1. It needs Multitap in Port 1 to be on. 'dualshock2' is the standard controller, which almost all games expect.\n\nIt is part of the machine. A game asks the console what is connected, so two projects that differ here are different machines and cannot share movies.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "ps2"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "port6",
      "display": "Controller 6 Type",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "dualshock2"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "What is plugged into slot B of the multitap in port 2. It needs Multitap in Port 2 to be on. 'dualshock2' is the standard controller, which almost all games expect.\n\nIt is part of the machine. A game asks the console what is connected, so two projects that differ here are different machines and cannot share movies.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "ps2"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "port7",
      "display": "Controller 7 Type",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "dualshock2"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "What is plugged into slot C of the multitap in port 2. It needs Multitap in Port 2 to be on. 'dualshock2' is the standard controller, which almost all games expect.\n\nIt is part of the machine. A game asks the console what is connected, so two projects that differ here are different machines and cannot share movies.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "ps2"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "port8",
      "display": "Controller 8 Type",
      "type": "enum",
      "options": [
        "none",
        "dualshock2"
      ],
      "default": "none",
      "description": "What is plugged into slot D of the multitap in port 2. It needs Multitap in Port 2 to be on. 'dualshock2' is the standard controller, which almost all games expect.\n\nIt is part of the machine. A game asks the console what is connected, so two projects that differ here are different machines and cannot share movies.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "ps2"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "multitap1",
      "display": "Multitap in Port 1",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": false,
      "description": "Whether a multitap is plugged into port 1. A multitap turns the port's one socket into four. A game can SEE the multitap whether or not anything is plugged into it, and controllers 3 to 5 and their memory cards can be reached only through it. That is why it is a setting of its own and is not worked out from the controllers. Otherwise a memory card in a slot would depend on a controller being in that slot.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "ps2"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "multitap2",
      "display": "Multitap in Port 2",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": false,
      "description": "Whether a multitap is plugged into port 2. A multitap turns the port's one socket into four. A game can SEE the multitap whether or not anything is plugged into it, and controllers 6 to 8 and their memory cards can be reached only through it. That is why it is a setting of its own and is not worked out from the controllers. Otherwise a memory card in a slot would depend on a controller being in that slot.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "ps2"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "memcard1",
      "display": "Memory Card 1",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": true,
      "description": "Whether there is a memory card in the console's slot 1. Games check on their first screen and behave differently without one, so it is part of the machine. The card's contents are save data. A card to start from goes in the project's Save Data as memcard1.ps2, and Emulator > Export Save Data writes it out under the same name.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "ps2"
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "memcard2",
      "display": "Memory Card 2",
      "type": "bool",
      "default": false,
      "description": "Whether there is a memory card in the console's slot 2. Games check on their first screen and behave differently without one, so it is part of the machine. The card's contents are save data. A card to start from goes in the project's Save Data as memcard2.ps2, and Emulator > Export Save Data writes it out under the same name.",
      "exposedWhen": {
        "setting": "machine",
        "is": "ps2"
      }
    },
